\details{
\subsection{Understanding the Results}{

\strong{Z-Statistics}: Based on the Wilcoxon signed-rank test for each pair
\itemize{
\item Large absolute Z values indicate big differences between two measurements
\item Z is computed from the differences \code{var2 - var1}, like
\code{wilcoxon_test(x = var1, y = var2)} and the SPSS pair
"var2 - var1"
\item As in SPSS, Z comes from the smaller rank sum and is never positive;
\code{z_based_on} names that sum (SPSS's footnote)
\item Based on negative ranks: values in var2 (Var 2) tend to be higher
\item Based on positive ranks: values in var1 (Var 1) tend to be higher
}

\strong{Adjusted P-values}: Control for multiple comparisons
\itemize{
\item p < 0.05: Measurements are significantly different
\item p >= 0.05: No significant difference between these measurements
}
}

\subsection{The Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Test}{

For each pair of measurements, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test:
\enumerate{
\item Computes differences between the two measurements
\item Ranks the absolute differences
\item Computes a Z-statistic based on the rank sums
\item Uses normal approximation with tie correction
}
}

\subsection{Which Cases Are Used}{

Each pair uses all cases with valid values on both of its variables
(pairwise deletion), exactly like the individual SPSS
\code{NPAR TESTS /WILCOXON} tests these results are validated against.
\code{friedman_test()} uses only cases complete on all variables
(listwise, as SPSS \code{/FRIEDMAN}), so a pair's \code{n} can be larger
than the Friedman N.
}

\subsection{P-Value Adjustment Methods}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{Bonferroni} (default): Most conservative, multiplies p by number of comparisons
\item \strong{Holm}: Step-down method, less conservative than Bonferroni
\item \strong{BH}: Controls false discovery rate, good for many comparisons
}
}

\subsection{When to Use This}{

Use pairwise Wilcoxon when:
\itemize{
\item Your Friedman test shows significant differences (p < 0.05)
\item You want to know which specific measurements differ
\item Your data are ordinal or violate normality assumptions
\item You have repeated measures or matched groups
}
}

\subsection{Relationship to Other Tests}{
\itemize{
\item Non-parametric post-hoc for repeated measures (like Dunn is for
independent groups)
\item Follow-up to \code{\link{friedman_test}()}, like \code{\link{dunn_test}()}
follows \code{\link{kruskal_wallis}()}
}
}

\subsection{Weighted variants}{

When the parent \code{\link{friedman_test}()} result is weighted, each
pairwise test uses the same frequency-weighted signed-rank formulas. SPSS
\code{NPAR TESTS} ignores \code{WEIGHT BY}, so weighted results have no
SPSS reference (R-only, guarded by an internal invariance suite); see
\code{vignette("spss-compatibility")} for validation status.
\itemize{
\item Each pairwise comparison uses \code{\link{wilcoxon_test}()} logic internally
}
}
